# NOTICE — provenance of everything in this package

## module/ — the kernel driver

`module/drivers/hid/intel-thc-hid` is the upstream Linux
`drivers/hid/intel-thc-hid` driver (7.3-rc generation sources, Intel
copyright headers intact, GPL-2.0-only) carrying the following changes, all
of them required for the Alder/Raptor Lake-L THC SPI ports:

1. **PCI IDs** for the ADL family SPI ports added to `quickspi_pci_tbl`:
   `8086:7AD8/7AD9` (ADL-S), `8086:51D0/51D1` (ADL-P — the Surface Laptop
   Studio 2), `8086:54D0/54D1` (ADL-M).
2. **ADL SPI opcode register layout** (`thc_spi_set_opcode_layout` /
   `thc_spi_encode_opcode`): this generation consumes the SPI opcodes
   differently from Meteor Lake — the opcode is replicated across the
   register's byte lanes, the DMA-read opcode register is skipped, and the
   write opcode is programmed to a different register (`0x1018`).
3. **`reset_on_resume`** driver data with the resume path resetting the
   touch controller on ADL platforms: on the Surface Laptop Studio 2 the
   firmware asserts the panel reset line from ACPI `_PS3` across suspend, so
   the controller comes back reset and must be re-initialised, not merely
   context-restored. The HID devices and their hidraw nodes persist across
   the reset; the controller returns in its default report mode.
4. **ACPI-first probe order**: ADL uses the same PCI IDs for HIDSPI and
   legacy mode, so the `_DSM` query runs before any hardware reconfiguration
   and a non-HIDSPI port fails probe cleanly.
5. **Interrupt completion re-check** (`thc_interrupt_finish`): THC interrupts
   are edge-triggered on the cause registers, so a cause latched while the
   handler runs raises no interrupt of its own; the handler now unmasks and
   re-checks before returning.
6. Small hardening/observability changes threaded through the tree (error
   logging on threaded-IRQ failure, initialised `*read_len` on an empty DMA
   ring).
7. **Forward-ported from upstream 7.3 final**: the output-report buffer is
   sized with `HIDSPI_OUTPUT_REPORT_SIZE()` and oversized reports are
   rejected in `write_cmd_to_txdma()` (upstream fix for the report-header
   overrun; three hunks, byte-identical to upstream).

**Where this tree comes from.** Items 1–6 are the source of the DKMS tree
`/usr/src/intel-thc-hid-0.1` on the reference Surface Laptop Studio 2 (Arch
Linux), a pre-existing community DKMS package whose author is not recorded.
That tree was built and installed with DKMS and validated on this machine
(touchpad, digitizer touch and pen). Item 7 is the upstream 7.3 fix
applied on top; the reference machine's installed DKMS tree does not
carry it yet.

**What is *not* claimed.** The ADL-S and ADL-M IDs (items 1) have not been
tested on any machine — they ship because the validated tree carries them;
expect the untested ones to behave the same, do not rely on it. Likewise the
`intel-quicki2c` module has no device on the Surface Laptop Studio 2: it is
built only to keep the three-module set at one consistent base (DKMS replaces
the in-tree modules by name). No proprietary code is included or
required.

## src/ — heatd and digid

Written for this project (MIT). The pad front's uhid descriptor is a
byte-exact collection of the device's own report descriptor
(`fixtures/ptp_collection_000D_0005.bin`, sha-pinned); the digitiser touch
front is the standard Windows 8 MT-class Touch Screen collection bound to
this device's geometry (its 0x06 response); the pen front keeps the
device's own pen collection verbatim.

`heatd-sources.sha256` pins the touchpad engine sources to the
hardware-validated revision; `make digid` refuses to link if they drift.

The processors link OpenCV (Apache-2.0) dynamically, for the segmentation
stage only (connected components and moments); no OpenCV sources are
bundled.

## udev rules, systemd units, scripts

Written for this project (MIT). They match the hardware by firmware-reported
identity only — never by hidraw number, which is assigned at enumeration and
can differ between boots. No component here assumes `iptsd`, `ithc`, or any other stack is
present or absent beyond the documented conflicts in README.md.

